=== task: multi easy
title: Jobs for opposite pointers
prompt: Choose **every** job that pointers from opposite ends do well.
* Reversing a list in place :: Right. Swap the ends and move both inward.
* Checking whether a word is a palindrome :: Right. Compare the ends and move both inward.
* Finding the two walls that hold the most water :: Right. Move the shorter wall inward.
* Finding two numbers in a sorted list that add up to a target :: Right. Move left when the sum is too small, right when it's too big.
- Removing every 0 from a list, keeping the order of the rest :: That's the same-direction style: a read pointer and a write pointer.
- Merging two sorted lists :: That uses one pointer in each list.
hint: Opposite pointers compare or swap the two ends of one list.
hint: Which jobs need the front and the back at the same time?
right: Reversing, palindromes, the most water and sorted pair sums all work from both ends inward. Removing items in place and merging use the other two styles.
wrong: Opposite pointers fit jobs that pair up the two ends of one list. In-place removal reads from the front, and merging walks two lists, so those use the other styles.

